# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  KONSTRYX — local development settings
#
#  Copy this file to .env and edit it. .env is gitignored and must stay that
#  way: it is for running the product on your own machine, nothing else.
#
#      copy .env.example .env
#
#  PRODUCTION CREDENTIALS DO NOT BELONG HERE. Once deployed, KONSTRYX resolves
#  S/4, SuccessFactors and Ariba credentials at runtime through the BTP
#  destination service and never sees them. A destination is configured once,
#  in the BTP cockpit, by whoever administers the subaccount. If you find
#  yourself pasting a live communication-user password into this file, that
#  password is in the wrong place.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# --- Local ports -----------------------------------------------------------
# The CAP service. 8090 rather than 8080, which the UI dev server uses.
KX_PORT=8090

# The UI dev server, which also proxies /odata to the service above.
KX_UI_PORT=8081


# --- Local sign-in ---------------------------------------------------------
# Which mock user the UI dev server signs in as. The service runs with mocked
# authentication locally; these users exist only in the default profile and
# mean nothing once deployed.
#
#   demo          every module, INFC, all projects   - general use
#   daud          Site Engineer + Resource Coordinator, INFC, PRJ-001 only
#                 - use this one to see project scoping actually filter
#   steward_infc  Master Data Steward, INFC
#   steward_pmi   Master Data Steward, PMI - shows master isolation
#   rohan         Cost Engineer, no company or project restriction
#   admin         bypasses the data-driven authorisation layer
KX_USER=demo
KX_PASS=demo


# --- SAPUI5 runtime --------------------------------------------------------
# The local SAPUI5 distribution served at /resources.
UI5_RUNTIME=C:\Users\Ziya\Documents\Claude\sapui5-rt-1.150.0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Everything below is RESERVED, not yet consumed by any code.
#  The S/4, SuccessFactors and Ariba connectors are not built - there is no
#  dev tenant to build them against (tracker Q-09). These keys are here so the
#  shape is agreed in advance; adding values now changes nothing.
#
#  When the connectors are built, the deployed application will read these
#  from BTP destinations named S4HANA_CLOUD, SUCCESSFACTORS and ARIBA. These
#  variables will only ever be a local-development convenience for pointing at
#  a sandbox tenant.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- S/4HANA Public Cloud --------------------------------------------------
# S4_HOST=https://myNNNNNN-api.s4hana.cloud.sap
# S4_CLIENT=100
# S4_USER=
# S4_PASSWORD=
# Prefer a client certificate over a password for anything but a sandbox:
# S4_CERT_PATH=
# S4_CERT_PASSWORD=

# --- SuccessFactors Employee Central ---------------------------------------
# SF_HOST=https://apiNN.successfactors.eu/odata/v2
# SF_COMPANY_ID=
# SF_CLIENT_KEY=
# SF_TOKEN_URL=

# --- Ariba (subcontract sourcing only) -------------------------------------
# ARIBA_HOST=
# ARIBA_REALM=
# ARIBA_API_KEY=

# --- Primavera P6 ----------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed where a client plans in P6 and the project comes into KONSTRYX
# rather than being created in it.
# P6_HOST=
# P6_USER=

# ===========================================================================
#  Local database - one word chooses, three settings follow
# ===========================================================================
#
#  KX_DB_MODE is the only line most people need. It stands for three settings
#  that have to agree with each other; set any of them yourself below and
#  yours wins.
#
#    memory   in-memory H2. The default. Nothing to install, identical on
#             every machine, and gone when the process stops. Most days this
#             is what you want.
#
#    fresh    local PostgreSQL, dropped and rebuilt on every start, with the
#             demo data reseeded behind it. A real database and real SQL,
#             with the same known dataset every morning.
#
#    keep     local PostgreSQL, left exactly as you left it. No schema, no
#             reseed. This is the mode where a row you write survives a
#             restart.
#
#  "keep" is the useful one and the one that goes wrong quietly. Read these
#  two before you use it:
#
#    - It creates nothing. The first run after a model change - a new entity,
#      a new column - must be "fresh", or the new table has nowhere to live
#      and the first read of it fails.
#    - It reseeds nothing. Demo data you have edited stays edited, including
#      edits you made by accident. When the numbers stop reconciling, run once
#      in "fresh" before believing the discrepancy.
#
#  The two Postgres modes need a database. Create it once, from the SQL Shell
#  (psql) that ships with PostgreSQL, or from pgAdmin:
#
#      CREATE DATABASE konstryx;
#
#  Then uncomment the three lines below and put your own password in. This
#  file is gitignored; application.yaml is not, which is why the password
#  lives here and not there.
#
KX_DB_MODE=memory
#
# KX_DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/konstryx
# KX_DB_USER=postgres
# KX_DB_PASSWORD=
#
#  Below this line nothing normally needs setting - KX_DB_MODE fills them in.
#  They are here because a mode is a convenience and not a cage: point the
#  platform at another database, or seed a kept one once, without waiting for
#  somebody to add a fourth mode.
#
# KX_DB_DRIVER=org.postgresql.Driver
# KX_DB_PLATFORM=postgres      h2 | postgres - picks schema-<platform>.sql
# KX_DB_INIT_MODE=always       never | embedded | always - run that schema
# KX_DB_SEED_MODE=always       never | embedded | always - load the CSVs
